\id SNG - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Song of Solomon 
\toc1 Song of Solomon 
\toc2 Song of Solomon 
\toc3 S. S. 
\mt1 SONG OF SOLOMON 
\c 1 
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* Song of Songs, which is Solomon's.
\q1
\v 2 Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth,
\q2 for your breasts are better than wine. 
\q1
\v 3 The fragrance of your perfumes is above all spices:
\q2 your name is perfume poured forth;
\q2 therefore the young maidens love you. 
\q1
\v 4 They have drawn you: 
\q2 we will run after you, after the fragrance of your perfumes:
\q2 the king has brought me into his chamber:
\q2 let us rejoice and be glad in you;
\q2 we will love your breasts more than wine:
\q2 uprightness loved you. 
\p
\q1
\v 5 I am dark and beautiful,
\q2 you daughters of Jerusalem,
\q2 as the tents of Kedar,
\q2 as the coverings of Solomon. 
\q1
\v 6 Don't look upon me, because I am dark,
\q2 because the sun has looked upon me:
\q2 my mother's sons strove with me;
\q2 they made me keeper in the vineyards;
\q2 I have not kept my own vineyard. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 Tell me, you whom my soul loves,
\q2 where you shepherd your flock,
\q2 where you rest at noon,
\q2 lest I become as one that is veiled
\q2 by the flocks of your companions. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 If you don't know yourself, you fair one among women,
\q2 go forth by the footsteps of the flocks,
\q2 and feed your kids by the shepherds' tents. 
\q1
\v 9 I have likened you, my companion,
\q2 to my mare in the chariots of Pharaoh. 
\q1
\v 10 How beautiful are your cheeks, like turtle-doves,
\q2 your neck as chains! 
\q1
\v 11 We will make you ornaments of gold
\q2 with studs of silver. 
\p
\q1
\v 12 While the king was reclining,
\q2 my spikenard gave forth its smell. 
\q1
\v 13 My beloved is to me a bundle of flowing myrrh;
\q2 he will lie between my breasts. 
\q1
\v 14 My beloved is to me a cluster of henna
\q2 in the vineyards of Engaddi. 
\p
\q1
\v 15 Behold, you are fair, my companion;
\q2 behold, you are fair;
\q2 your eyes are doves. 
\q1
\v 16 Behold, you are fair, my beloved,
\q2 yes, beautiful,
\q2 indeed, our couch is shaded. 
\q1
\v 17 The beams of our house are cedars,
\q2 our ceilings are of cypress. 
\c 2 
\p
\q1
\v 1 I am a flower of the plain,
\q2 a lily of the valleys. 
\p
\q1
\v 2 As a lily among thorns,
\q2 so is my companion among the daughters. 
\p
\q1
\v 3 As the apple among the trees of the wood,
\q2 so is my beloved among the sons.
\q1 I desired his shadow, and sat down,
\q2 and his fruit was sweet in my throat. 
\q1
\v 4 Bring me into the wine house;
\q2 set love before me. 
\q1
\v 5 Strengthen me with \f + \fr 2:5 \fqa Gr. \ft ἀμόραις, a rare Greek word of uncertain meaning.\f*amora-cakes,
\q2 stay me with apples:
\q2 for I \add am\add* wounded with love. 
\q1
\v 6 His left hand is under my head,
\q2 and his right hand will embrace me. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 I have charged you, you daughters of Jerusalem,
\q2 by the powers and by the strengths of the field,
\q2 that you do not rouse or wake \add my\add* love,
\q2 until he pleases. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 The voice of my beloved!
\q2 Behold, he comes
\q2 leaping over the mountains,
\q2 bounding over the hills. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 My beloved is like a gazelle or a young hart on the mountains of Bethel:
\q2 behold, he is behind our wall,
\q2 looking through the windows,
\q2 peeping through the lattices. 
\q1
\v 10 My beloved answers,
\q2 and says to me, Rise up;
\q2 come, my companion, my fair one, my dove. 
\q1
\v 11 For, behold, the winter is past,
\q2 the rain is gone,
\q2 it has departed. 
\q1
\v 12 The flowers are seen in the land;
\q2 the time of pruning has arrived;
\q2 the voice of the turtle-dove has been heard in our land. 
\q1
\v 13 The fig-tree has put forth its young figs,
\q2 the vines are \f + \fr 2:13 \fqa Gr. \ft κυπρίζουσιν, a rare Greek verb, probably referring to vines in blossom or early fruit.\f*budding,
\q2 they yield a fragrance:
\q2 arise, come, my companion, my fair one, my dove;
\q2 yes, come. 
\p
\q1
\v 14 Come, my dove, in the shelter of the rock,
\q2 beside the wall:
\q2 show me your face,
\q2 and cause me to hear your voice;
\q2 for your voice is sweet,
\q2 and your face is beautiful. 
\p
\q1
\v 15 Take us the little foxes that spoil the vines:
\q2 for our vines are \f + \fr 2:15 \fqa Gr. \ft κυπρίζουσιν, a rare Greek verb, probably referring to vines in blossom or early fruit.\f*budding. 
\p
\q1
\v 16 My beloved is mine, and I am his:
\q2 he feeds \add his flock\add* among the lilies. 
\p
\q1
\v 17 Until the day breathes, and the shadows move,
\q2 turn, my beloved,
\q2 be like a gazelle or young hart on the mountains of the ravines. 
\c 3 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Night after night on my bed
\q2 I sought him whom my soul loves:
\q2 I sought him, but didn't find him;
\q2 I called him, but he didn't listen to me. 
\q1
\v 2 I will rise now, and go about in the city,
\q2 in the market-places, and in the streets,
\q2 and I will seek him whom my soul loves:
\q2 I sought him, but I didn't find him. 
\q1
\v 3 The watchmen who go their rounds in the city found me.
\q2 \add I said\add*, Have you seen him whom my soul loves? 
\q1
\v 4 Scarcely had I parted from them,
\q2 when I found him whom my soul loves:
\q2 I held him, and did not let him go,
\q2 until I brought him into my mother's house,
\q2 and into the chamber of her who conceived me. 
\p
\q1
\v 5 I have charged you, you daughters of Jerusalem,
\q2 by the powers and by the strengths of the field,
\q2 that you do not rouse or wake \add my\add* love,
\q2 until he pleases. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 Who is this that comes up from the wilderness like pillars of smoke,
\q2 perfumed with myrrh and frankincense,
\q2 with every fragrant powder of the perfumer? 
\q1
\v 7 Behold Solomon's bed;
\q2 sixty mighty men of the mighty ones of Israel are all around it. 
\q1
\v 8 They all hold a sword, being expert in war:
\q2 every man \add has\add* his sword upon his thigh
\q2 because of fear by night. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 King Solomon made himself a carriage
\q2 from the wood of Lebanon. 
\q1
\v 10 He made its pillars silver,
\q2 its seat gold,
\q2 its step scarlet,
\q2 its middle a pavement of love,
\q2 from the daughters of Jerusalem. 
\q1
\v 11 Go forth, you daughters of Zion, and behold King Solomon,
\q2 with the crown with which his mother crowned him,
\q2 in the day of his wedding,
\q2 and in the day of the gladness of his heart. 
\c 4 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Behold, you are fair, my companion;
\q2 behold, you are fair;
\q2 your eyes are doves, beside your veil:
\q2 your hair is as flocks of goats,
\q2 that have appeared from Gilead. 
\q1
\v 2 Your teeth are as flocks of shorn \add sheep\add*,
\q2 that have gone up from the washing;
\q2 all of them bearing twins,
\q2 and there isn't a barren one among them. 
\q1
\v 3 Your lips are as a thread of scarlet,
\q2 and your speech is lovely:
\q2 like the rind of a pomegranate is your cheek without your veil. 
\q1
\v 4 Your neck is as the tower of David,
\q2 that was built for an armoury:
\q2 a thousand shields hang upon it,
\q2 all darts of mighty men. 
\q1
\v 5 Your two breasts are as two twin fawns,
\q2 that feed among the lilies. 
\q1
\v 6 Until the day breathes, and the shadows move,
\q2 I will go to the mountain of myrrh,
\q2 and to the hill of frankincense. 
\q1
\v 7 You are all fair, my companion,
\q2 and there is no spot in you. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 Come from Lebanon, my bride,
\q2 come from Lebanon:
\q2 you will come and pass from the top of \f + \fr 4:8 \fqa Gr. \ft "Faith." The Hebrew text has the place-name Amanah, which the Septuagint appears to translate rather than transliterate.\f*Faith,
\q2 from the top of Sanir and Hermon,
\q2 from the lions' dens,
\q2 from the mountains of the leopards. 
\q1
\v 9 You \f + \fr 4:9 \fqa Gr. \ft ἐκαρδίωσας, an apparent neologism formed from the noun “heart”; the English “hearted” attempts to reflect the Greek’s unusual word formation.\f*hearted us, my sister, my bride;
\q2 you hearted us with one of your eyes,
\q2 with one chain of your neck. 
\q1
\v 10 How beautiful are your breasts, my sister, my bride!
\q2 How much more beautiful are your breasts than wine,
\q2 and the fragrance of your garments than all spices! 
\q1
\v 11 Your lips drop honeycomb, my bride:
\q2 honey and milk are under your tongue;
\q2 and the fragrance of your garments is as the fragrance of Lebanon. 
\q1
\v 12 My sister, my bride, is a garden enclosed;
\q2 a garden enclosed, a fountain sealed. 
\q1
\v 13 Your shoots are a garden of pomegranates,
\q2 with the choice fruits;
\q2 henna, with spikenard: 
\q1
\v 14 spikenard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon;
\q2 with all the wood of Lebanon,
\q2 myrrh, aloes, with all chief spices: 
\q1
\v 15 a fountain of a garden,
\q2 and a well of water springing and gurgling from Lebanon. 
\p
\q1
\v 16 Awake, O north wind; and come, O south;
\q2 and blow through my garden,
\q2 and let my spices flow out. 
\c 5 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Let my beloved come down into his garden,
\q2 and eat his choice fruit.
\q2 I have come into my garden, my sister, my bride:
\q2 I have gathered my myrrh with my spices;
\q2 I have eaten my bread with my honey;
\q2 I have drunk my wine with my milk.
\q2 Eat, O friends, and drink;
\q2 yes, brothers, drink and become drunk. 
\p
\q1
\v 2 I sleep, but my heart is awake:
\q2 the voice of my beloved! He knocks at the door,
\q2 \add saying\add*, Open, open to me,
\q2 my companion, my sister, my dove, my perfect one:
\q2 for my head is filled with dew,
\q2 and my hair with the drops of the night. 
\q1
\v 3 I have put off my garment;
\q2 how will I put it on?
\q2 I have washed my feet,
\q2 how will I defile them? 
\q1
\v 4 My beloved put forth his hand by the hole \add of the door\add*,
\q2 and my belly was moved for him. 
\q1
\v 5 I rose up to open to my beloved;
\q2 my hands dropped myrrh,
\q2 my fingers full of choice myrrh,
\q2 on the handles of the lock. 
\q1
\v 6 I opened to my beloved;
\q2 my beloved had gone:
\q2 my soul went out at his word.
\q2 I sought him, but didn't find him;
\q2 I called him, but he didn't answer me. 
\q1
\v 7 The watchmen who go their rounds in the city found me,
\q2 they struck me, they wounded me;
\q2 the keepers of the walls took away my veil from me. 
\q1
\v 8 I have charged you, O daughters of Jerusalem,
\q2 by the powers and the strengths of the field:
\q2 if you find my beloved,
\q2 what are you to say to him?
\q2 That I am wounded with love. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 What is your beloved \add more\add* than \add another\add* beloved,
\q2 O beautiful among women?
\q2 What is your beloved \add more\add* than \add another\add* beloved,
\q2 that you have so charged us? 
\p
\q1
\v 10 My beloved is fair and ruddy,
\q2 chosen out from myriads. 
\q1
\v 11 His head is \add as\add* very fine gold,
\q2 his locks are flowing, black as a raven. 
\q1
\v 12 His eyes are as doves,
\q2 by the pools of waters,
\q2 washed with milk,
\q2 sitting by the pools of waters. 
\q1
\v 13 His cheeks are as bowls of spices
\q2 pouring forth perfumes:
\q2 his lips are lilies,
\q2 dropping choice myrrh. 
\q1
\v 14 His hands are as worked gold set with beryl:
\q2 his belly is an ivory tablet on a sapphire stone. 
\q1
\v 15 His legs are marble pillars set on golden sockets:
\q2 his form is as Lebanon,
\q2 choice as the cedars. 
\q1
\v 16 His throat is most sweet,
\q2 and altogether desirable.
\q2 This is my beloved,
\q2 and this is my companion,
\q2 O daughters of Jerusalem. 
\c 6 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Where has your beloved gone, you beautiful among women?
\q2 Where has your beloved turned aside?
\q2 \add tell us\add*, and we will seek him with you. 
\p
\q1
\v 2 My beloved is gone down to his garden,
\q2 to the beds of spice,
\q2 to feed \add his flock\add* in the gardens,
\q2 and to gather lilies. 
\q1
\v 3 I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine,
\q2 who feeds among the lilies. 
\p
\q1
\v 4 You are fair, my companion, as \f + \fr 6:4 \fqa Gr. \ft "Pleasure." Where the Hebrew has the place-name Tirzah, the Septuagint has this abstract noun.\f*Pleasure,
\q2 beautiful as Jerusalem,
\q2 fearsome as \add armies\add* set in array. 
\q1
\v 5 Turn away your eyes from before me,
\q2 for they have given me wings:
\q2 your hair is as flocks of goats which have appeared from Gilead. 
\q1
\v 6 Your teeth are as flocks of shorn \add sheep\add*,
\q2 that have gone up from the washing, all of them bearing twins,
\q2 and there is no one barren among them:
\q2 your lips are as a thread of scarlet,
\q2 and your speech is lovely. 
\q1
\v 7 Your cheek is like the rind of a pomegranate,
\q2 outside your veil. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 There are sixty queens, and eighty concubines,
\q2 and maidens without number. 
\q1
\v 9 My dove, my perfect one, is one;
\q2 she is the \add only\add* one of her mother;
\q2 she is the choice of her that bore her.
\q2 The daughters saw her, and the queens will call her blessed,
\q2 yes, and the concubines, and they will praise her. 
\q1
\v 10 Who is this that looks forth as the morning,
\q2 fair as the moon, choice as the sun,
\q2 fearsome as \add armies\add* set in array? 
\p
\q1
\v 11 I went down to the garden of nuts,
\q2 to look at the fruits of the stream,
\q2 to see if the vine flowered, 
\q2\add if\add* the pomegranates blossomed. 
\q1
\v 12 There I will give you my breasts.
\q2 My soul didn't know.
\q2 It made me as the chariots of Aminadab. 
\c 7 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Return, return, O Shulamite;
\q2 return, return, and we will look at you. 
\p What will you see in the Shulamite?
\q2 She who comes like the dances of armies. 
\p
\q1
\v 2 Your steps are beautiful in shoes,
\q2 O daughter of Nadab:
\q2 the curves of \add your\add* thighs are like chains,
\q2 the work of the craftsman. 
\q1
\v 3 Your navel is a worked bowl,
\q2 never lacking mixed wine;
\q2 your belly is a heap of wheat
\q2 set about with lilies. 
\q1
\v 4 Your two breasts are as two twin fawns. 
\q1
\v 5 Your neck is as an ivory tower;
\q2 your eyes are as pools in Heshbon,
\q2 by the gates of the daughter of many:
\q2 your nose is as the tower of Lebanon,
\q2 looking toward Damascus. 
\q1
\v 6 Your head upon you is as Carmel,
\q2 and the curls of your hair like scarlet;
\q2 the king is bound in the galleries. 
\q1
\v 7 How beautiful you are,
\q2 and how sweet you are, my love! 
\q1
\v 8 This is your stature in your delights:
\q2 you were likened to a palm tree,
\q2 and your breasts to clusters. 
\q1
\v 9 I said, I will go up to the palm tree,
\q2 I will take hold of its high boughs:
\q2 and now will your breasts be as clusters of the vine,
\q2 and the fragrance of your nose as apples; 
\q1
\v 10 and your throat as good wine,
\q2 going straight with my beloved,
\q2 suiting my lips and teeth. 
\p
\q1
\v 11 I am my beloved's,
\q2 and his turning is toward me. 
\q1
\v 12 Come, my beloved, let us go forth into the field;
\q2 let us lodge in the villages. 
\q1
\v 13 Let us go early into the vineyards;
\q2 let us see if the vine has flowered,
\q2 \add if\add* the budding has appeared,
\q2 if the pomegranates have blossomed;
\q2 there I will give you my breasts. 
\q1
\v 14 The mandrakes have given a fragrance,
\q2 and at our doors \add are\add* all kinds of choice fruits,
\q2 new and old.
\q2 O my beloved, I have kept \add them\add* for you. 
\c 8 
\p
\q1
\v 1 I wish you, O my beloved, were one who had sucked my mother's breasts;
\q2 when I found you outside, I would kiss you;
\q2 yes, they should not despise me. 
\q1
\v 2 I would take you, I would bring you into my mother's house,
\q2 and into the chamber of her who conceived me;
\q2 I would make give you spiced wine to drink,
\q2 of the juice of my pomegranates. 
\p
\q1
\v 3 His left hand is under my head, 
\q2 and his right hand will embrace me. 
\p
\q1
\v 4 I have charged you, you daughters of Jerusalem,
\q2 by the powers and the strengths of the field,
\q2 why should you rouse, and why should you wake \add my\add* love
\q2 until he pleases?
\p
\q1
\v 5 Who is this who comes up all white,
\q2 leaning on her beloved?
\q2 I raised you up under an apple-tree;
\q2 there your mother brought you forth;
\q2 there the one who bore you brought you forth. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 Set me as a seal upon your heart,
\q2 as a seal upon your arm;
\q2 for love is as strong as death;
\q2 jealousy is as cruel as the grave,
\q2 its shafts are shafts of fire,
\q2 \add even\add* the flames of it. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 Much water will not be able to quench love,
\q2 and rivers will not drown it;
\q2 if a man would give all he has for love,
\q2 they would completely despise it. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 Our sister is little, and has no breasts;
\q2 what will we do for our sister,
\q2 in the day in which she will be spoken for? 
\q1
\v 9 If she is a wall, let us build upon her silver bulwarks;
\q2 and if she is a door, let us carve for her cedar panels. 
\q1
\v 10 I am a wall, and my breasts are as towers;
\q2 I was in his eyes as one who found peace. 
\q1
\v 11 Solomon had a vineyard in Baal Hamon;
\q2 he leased his vineyard to keepers;
\q2 every one was to bring for its fruit a thousand \add pieces\add* of silver. 
\q1
\v 12 My vineyard, even mine, is before me;
\q2 Solomon \add will have\add* a thousand,
\q2 and those who keep its fruit two hundred. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 You who dwell in the gardens,
\q2 the companions listen to your voice:
\q2 make me hear \add it\add*. 
\p
\q1
\v 14 Flee, my beloved,
\q2 and be like a doe or a fawn on the mountains of spices. 
